267:
(a) Definitions. The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this section, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Charitable organization means any person, group or organization which complies with Georgia Charitable Solicitations Act of 19B8, O.C.G.A. § 43-17-1 et seq.
Educational institution means an entity organized and operated exclusively for educational purposes and which either maintains a regular faculty and curriculum and has a regularly enrolled body of students in attendance at the place where its educational activities are regularly carried on, or is accredited by a nationally recognized, independent higher education accreditation body.
Peddler shall mean any person who engages in the business of selling any goods, wares, merchandise or services or who solicits orders for such goods, wares, merchandise or services by going from house to house or place to place and who does not have a permanent place of business.
Religious organization means an entity which conducts regular worship services or is qualified as a religious organization under Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as now or hereafter amended, that is not required to file IRS Form 990, Return of Organization Exempt From Income Tax, under any circumstances.
Roadside stand whenever used in this section, means any trailer, tent, motor vehicle, or other facility or structure, not erected on a foundation that is erected and operated for the sale or solicitation of any merchandise. No roadside stand may be used as a food establishment as defined in O.C.G.A. § 26-2-370.
Soliciting as it applies to this section, shall mean any action which compels or attempts to compel another to purchase, donate or otherwise do business with the person doing the soliciting.
Temporary merchant is a person who sells produce, goods and merchandise for profit, from a stationary location or from a pushcart, bicycle cart, tent, motorized cart or any other motor vehicle or trailer, that is temporary in nature (six months or less in one location), and is not operated by or as an accessory use to the primary use existing on the premises.
Vendor means a roadside vendor or any person who sells, gives away through barter, offers to sell, or solicits to sell any fruit, vegetables, nuts, berries, citrus, cut flowers or any other similar item.
(b) Registration required. Any person 18 years of age or older who is peddling, soliciting or canvassing within the city, shall be required to register and obtain an identification card as provided by this article.
(c) Registration; fee; application for identification card. Any person 18 years of age or older desiring to sell, peddle, solicit or canvass within the city shall make application with the city through its designated agent and pay a nonrefundable registration fee of $25.00. The application shall provide the following information:
(1) Name and description of applicant;
(2) Address, both legal and local;
(3) A brief description of the nature of the business and the goods to be sold;
(4) The name and address of the applicant's employer, if other than the applicant, together with credentials establishing the exact relationship between the applicant and the employer (i.e. date of hire, position applicant holds);
(5) If a vehicle is used, a description of the vehicle and tag number, as well as a copy of the driver's license of the designated driver;
(6) Information on any convictions or pending cases of any crime, misdemeanor or violation of any municipal ordinance, the nature of the offense and the punishment or penalty assessed therefore;
(7) The date(s) and time(s) which the applicant proposes to sell, peddle, solicit or canvass within the city; and
(8) Anticipated location of selling, peddling or solicitation.
(d) Investigation of applicants; consideration of approval. Eligibility of an applicant is contingent upon an investigation which reveals the applicant's history is free from convictions, charges or accusations of a nature which, in the judgment of a reasonable person, could jeopardize the safety and property of the citizens and of the city in general. Such convictions, charges and accusations include, but are not limited to:
(1) Felonies;
(2) Misdemeanors of a high and aggravated nature;
(3) Misdemeanors which would constitute moral turpitude.
After proper inquiry and investigation, the police department shall determine whether the applicant is eligible to be permitted. Each eligible applicant shall be issued a registration certificate. If, after proper inquiry and investigation, the police department shall determine that the applicant is not eligible, the police department shall deny the application and file the application, investigation documentation and written recommendation to deny the applicant at the police department where it will be maintained for two years; and, notify the applicant by personal service or by first-class mail to the address shown on the application. It shall be conclusively presumed that notice by first-class mail was received by the applicant and the failure of the applicant to receive such notice shall not invalidate the action taken.
(e) Exhibition of registration/identification card. Each registrant under this article shall at all times while in the city visibly display the registration and shall exhibit the same when requested to do so by any law enforcement officer or by any municipal authority and by any person being solicited. Possession of this registration card shall not in any way represent an endorsement or approval of any product or project by the city. No registration card shall be used at any time by any person other than the one to whom it is issued.
(f) Unlawful acts. It shall be unlawful for any person to sell, peddle, canvass or solicit after sundown. It shall be unlawful for any person to sell, peddle, canvass or solicit without having registered with the police department in accordance with this article or to sell, peddle, solicit or canvass without having on his person and in his possession the registration issued in accordance with this article. It shall be unlawful for any person to alter or deface any permit or registration issued under this article. Violation of this section shall result in citation and punishment as set forth in the general provisions chapter of this Code. It shall be unlawful for any person to sell, peddle, canvass or solicit who is under the age of 18 years of age. It shall be unlawful for any person to sell or peddle food items prepared on site or which are otherwise prepared outside of a state-licensed facility, except for the following conditions:
City-sponsored or approved festivals (for city-sponsored or approved festivals or special events the vendor must obtain permission from the city or its designee to set up a booth and must pay the vendor fee for the event);
Charitable fundraisers on private property provided the property owner has given written consent.
It shall be unlawful for any person to sell, peddle, canvass or solicit on public property or within the right-of-way in the city limits including, but not limited to:
State Route 27.
State Route 34.
State Route 100.
It shall be unlawful for any vendor or itinerant vendor to operate within or upon, and sell, solicit, or barter, transact or solicit business without complying with the following requirements;
(1) Possession of a valid permit, issued by the police department. This requirement shall not apply to those persons excepted by subsection (g) below;
(2) Must be located on privately owned real property within the commercial, industrial, or highway frontage zoning districts, and with the written permission of the owner of the property;
(3) Must comply with all other ordinances of the city specifically including sign ordinances and parking requirements;
(4) Must be located at least thirty-five feet back from the paved edge of any roadway, and, if operated on a corner lot, shall not be operated within a sight distance triangle area formed by the street, property lines, and a line connecting them at points at least 35 feet from the intersection of the street lines maintaining at least 35 feet from any intersection. The measurement of 35 feet shall start at the edge of the roadway, inward;
(5) There shall be a maximum of one vendor per business location;
(6) There shall be a maximum of five vendors permits issued per day within the city.
(g) Exceptions. The fee set forth in subsection (c) above shall not apply to:
(1) Solicitations, sales or distributions made by educational, civic, religious or charitable organizations which have their principal place of activity in the city or which are otherwise approved by the mayor and council;
(2) Persons selling, distributing or soliciting orders for farm and garden produce as raised or prepared by the vendor or members of his family, and homegrown so far as the sale of such commodities is authorized by law;
(3) Persons selling, distributing or soliciting orders for local or area newspapers.
All persons and organizations as described in subsections (1), (2), and (3) above shall comply with all other requirements of this section.
(h) Standards and guidelines for granting or denying approval. In addition to all other requirements stated in this chapter, the city shall consider the following standards and guidelines in reaching its decision on whether to grant or deny an application to peddle, solicit, canvass, or sell as a temporary merchant:
(1) The report of the police investigation as required by this chapter, and any other reports that may be required;
(2) The number of approvals already granted for similar entities within the city limits and in the trading area of the location for which an approval is sought;
(3) The criminal record, general good character and reputation of the applicant and/or the affiliated organization;
(4) Whether the location would tend to increase and promote traffic congestion and resulting hazards therefrom;
(5) The nature of the neighborhood immediately adjacent to the proposed location, that is, whether the neighborhood is predominantly residential, industrial or business;
(6) Whether the applicant has satisfied all requirements pertaining to the application process;
(7) Consideration of the health, safety, location, parking and welfare of the citizens and preservation of surrounding neighborhoods, with reference to such factors as noise, traffic and loitering;
(8) Consideration of the health, safety, and welfare of the participants to the selling, peddling, soliciting and canvassing, including, but not limited to, age of participants, level of adult supervision, likelihood of subjection of participants to life threatening occurrences; and
(9) The history or reputation of the location for criminal activity, including, but not limited to, prostitution or other sex offenses, crimes of violence, gambling, and illegal dealing in alcoholic beverages or drugs.
(i) Temporary merchants. A temporary merchant shall comply with the following rules:
(1) Display of products must be located outside of the road right-of-way, and all structures shall be located in accordance with the setback requirements based on the zoning district and classification of the property on which the activity is taking place.
(2) May only be located on property where controlled vehicular access and adequate parking located outside of the road right-of-way is provided.
(3) Meet criteria established by the standard building code for construction for any temporary structure on the premises, other than a tent. A building permit will be required for such structures.
(4) Provide to the city a copy of a written agreement of the land owner permitting use of the property by the roadside vendor, accompanied by a plat of the property being used, if the temporary merchant is not the owner of the property on which the activity is taking place.
(5) Comply with all City Code requirements, including site plan review and building plan review, upon erection of any structure requiring electrical service, temporary or permanent.
(6) Comply with all requirements of this chapter relating to signs. The signs of temporary merchants will be treated as temporary signs for special events and shall meet the requirements set forth in the city's sign ordinance. The temporary sign may be maintained for a maximum period of six months.
A temporary merchant must obtain a permit from the city, and pay the occupation tax or registration fee prior to engaging in any selling or display activities set forth in this section.
If the temporary merchant does not construct a building or structure in compliance with the city building codes, then any tent, stand, or other temporary structure must be removed from the site at dusk during each day of operation.
Any temporary stand or tent must be maintained in a neat and orderly manner free of loose debris and trash. Proper trash receptacles should be provided and maintained.
(Ord. of 5-4-2017(1), Exh. A)
Editor's note— Ordinance adopted Feb. 2, 2017(1), Exh. A, repealed § 16-45 in its entirety and enacted a new § 16-45 to read as set out herein. Former § 16-45 pertained to peddlers and itinerant merchants and derived from Ordinance adopted Nov. 5, 2015.

